      {
        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "aa96392c-43ea-51d7-81ad-a59339589c28",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Places: New York State, Quebec, Catskill Mountains, Lake Huron, New England, Laurentia, North America\n- Concepts: Queenston Delta, Taconic orogeny, Deicke and Millbrig bentonite layers, K-bentonite, Late Ordovician\n\nFacts:\n- The Taconic orogeny was a mountain-building period that ended 440 million years ago and affected most of modern-day New England.\n- During the Late Ordovician on Laurentia, the craton of North America, a volcanic eruption during the Taconic orogeny formed the Deicke and Millbrig bentonite layers, including the potassium bentonite layer called K-bentonite.\n- The Queenston Delta wedge is thickest in a band running from New York State to Quebec and extends from the Catskill Mountains to Lake Huron.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
